_wood.jpg/500px-Sequoia_wood.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Sequoia_wood.jpg/500px-Sequoia_wood.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The wood of coast redwood is distinctively red.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASequoia_wood.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/BlkWalnut-x-section.jpg/330px-BlkWalnut-x-section.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/BlkWalnut-x-section.jpg/330px-BlkWalnut-x-section.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Magnified cross-section of black walnut, showing the vessels, medullary rays (white lines) and annual rings: this is intermediate between diffuse-porous and ring-porous, with vessel size declining gradually","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlkWalnut-x-section.jpg"}],"sounds":{"briar":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6c/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-briar.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/6/6c/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-briar.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-briar.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/6/6c/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-briar.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-briar.wav.ogg"}],"wood":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/En-au-wood.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/6/65/En-au-wood.ogg/En-au-wood.ogg.mp3"}]}}}